\subsection{The dimension profile needs a distance hypothesis}
The primary source already proves properties of the theta profiles and gives a Gabidulin characterization with minimum rank distance greater than one. We test whether the profile alone can replace that distance hypothesis. Let $F=\mathbb F_2[\alpha]/(\alpha^3+\alpha+1)$; the cubic has no root in $\mathbb F_2$, hence is irreducible. Take $\theta(z)=z^2$ and the two $F$-linear length-three codes
\[
C=\{x:x_1+\alpha x_2+\alpha^2x_3=0\},\qquad
C'=\{x:x_1+\alpha x_2=0\}.
\]
\begin{proposition}
Both codes have the same theta-dimension profile $(2,3,3,\ldots)$, but $C$ has minimum rank distance 2 and is MRD, while $C'$ has minimum rank distance 1.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each is the kernel of one nonzero linear functional, so has dimension two. Its theta image has normal obtained by squaring each original normal coordinate. That new normal is not a scalar multiple of the original: their first coordinates are both 1, forcing any scalar multiple to use scalar 1, while $\alpha^2\ne\alpha$. Thus the code and its theta image are distinct two-dimensional subspaces of $F^3$, whose sum is all of $F^3$. This proves both profiles.
A nonzero rank-one vector in $F^3$ is of the form $\lambda v$ with $\lambda\ne0$ and $v\in\mathbb F_2^3\setminus\{0\}$, because its coordinates span a one-dimensional $\mathbb F_2$-space. Such a vector cannot lie in $C$: $v_1+\alpha v_2+\alpha^2v_3\ne0$ by linear independence of $1,\alpha,\alpha^2$. The codeword $(\alpha,1,0)$ lies in $C$ and has rank weight 2, so its minimum is exactly 2, equal to $n-k+1$. In contrast $e_3\in C'$ has rank weight 1, proving the second claim.
\end{proof}
For completeness, the standard nonincreasing-increment property also has a short direct proof. With $S_i=\sum_{j=0}^i\theta^j(C)$, for $i\ge1$ the map induced by $\theta$ from $S_i/S_{i-1}$ onto $S_{i+1}/S_i$ is well defined, since $\theta(S_{i-1})\subseteq S_i$, and is surjective because $S_{i+1}=C+\theta(S_i)$ and $C\subseteq S_i$. This semilinear surjection cannot increase dimension, so $\Delta_i\le\Delta_{i-1}$. These necessary profile properties do not force a desired minimum distance, as the example shows.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
All 64 codewords of each code were enumerated over the explicitly represented field, their binary rank weights computed, and their theta-sum dimensions checked. The failed route is declaring a candidate MRD solely from this dimension profile. The example does not refute a characterization restricted in advance to MRD codes, nor the source's theorem with distance greater than one: $C'$ violates that hypothesis. No new MRD family or classification among already-MRD families is obtained.

